  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on a qualitative and survey-based case study of a single rapidly urbanizing city in the Global South, which may limit generalizability to contexts with stronger legal frameworks.",
      "what": "This study examines how migrant renters construct and experience the concept of home in Kathmandu, Nepal, where approximately half of the population consists of rural-to-urban migrants. Using ethnographic research and survey data, the study analyzes how the virtual absence of legal tenancy regulations and the lack of legal recognition for renters shape daily life.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that when tenancy relations are unregulated, renters internalize their housing precarity by viewing their urban homes as inherently temporary and unstable, shifting the understanding of homemaking from a process of settlement to one of managed transience."
    }
  },
